-- title:
--   Shrinking commutes with translation
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a normed additive commutative group, let $S \subseteq E$, let $r \in \mathbb{R}$ and let $x \in E$. Writing $S[x] = \{\,y : y - x \in S\,\} = x + S$ and $S_r = \{\,y \in S : \bar B_r(y) \subseteq S\,\}$, one has
--   $$\bigl(S[x]\bigr)_r \;=\; \bigl(S_r\bigr)[x] .$$
--
--   Shrinking and translating commute, as they must, since the clearance condition defining the shrinking is invariant under translation. The identity is what lets a shrunk cone based at a point be read either as "shrink, then move" or "move, then shrink". No sign hypothesis is imposed on $r$: the statement holds for every real $r$, including negative ones, where the closed ball is empty and the shrinking is $S$ itself.
-- source:
